    {
      "text": "The other day, we had this meeting that was supposed to last 30 minutes. It ended up going for almost an hour and a half. At first, I was fine, focused, taking notes, trying to stay in the loop. But once we hit that 30-minute mark, I could feel myself checking out. My mind started drifting to the work piling up, the emails waiting, and honestly, just how much I wanted to stand up and stretch. There was this one guy who kept saying, \"Just a quick point,\" and every time, it turned into another 10 minutes. I remember glancing at the clock and thinking, \"We could've wrapped this up ages ago.\" But no one wanted to be the one to say it. By the time it finally ended, everyone looked exhausted. That fake smile, that, \"All right, thanks everyone\" tone we all use when we're just done. I closed my laptop, leaned back, and thought, \"We really need to learn how to end things on time.\" It's not even about being impatient. It's about focus. Once people start thinking about lunch or the next task, you've already lost the room. A good meeting leaves people clear and ready to move. A long one, it just drains the energy out of everyone.",
      "start_time": 0.199,
      "end_time": 78.719,
